WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.017 --> 00:00:09.338
- Let me introduce myself.

00:00:09.338 --> 00:00:10.737
I'm Jim Levinsohn,

00:00:10.737 --> 00:00:12.563
Director of the Jackson Institute,

00:00:13.681 --> 00:00:16.493
soon to be the Yale Jackson
School of Global Affairs.

00:00:17.697 --> 00:00:19.043
I'd like to welcome everybody.

00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:21.270
Special welcome, we're joined today

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.023
by a number of the Ukrainian
students here at Yale.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.923
Our thoughts, prayers.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.720
It's hard for me to imagine
what you are going through

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.138
as you are here in New Haven

00:00:37.138 --> 00:00:39.113
and dealing with the situation at home.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.143
So thank you for joining us.

00:00:43.494 --> 00:00:47.193
I'm joined also by two of
my colleagues up front here,

00:00:48.138 --> 00:00:49.240
a number also in the audience.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.410
Let me introduce them.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:56.410
Arne Westad, Professor of
History and Global Affairs.

00:00:57.461 --> 00:00:59.997
Arne has written an amazing book,

00:00:59.997 --> 00:01:02.550
"History of the Cold War,"

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.140
and has written on and
continues to write on

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.073
great power competition.

00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:11.180
I'm also to joined by Tim Snyder.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.810
If you have walked by a
bookstore, turned on the radio,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.240
read a newspaper, watched television,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.143
you know who Tim probably,
you probably know who Tim is.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.020
We've had TV crews camped
out on the first floor

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.103
of Horchow Hall now for quite some time.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.283
Tim has been working
an incredible schedule.

00:01:33.471 --> 00:01:35.221
Very, very grateful that he's here.

00:01:36.574 --> 00:01:40.498
What you might not know,
if you've read, you know,

00:01:40.498 --> 00:01:44.490
only his most recent work
is that Tim first really,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.620
you know, his earlier work as a historian

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.370
before he was a public intellectual

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:53.633
as well as historian was a
book called "Bloodlands,"

00:01:54.552 --> 00:01:58.710
which is a history of exactly the area

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.923
that we'll be talking about
today in the post Stalin era.

00:02:03.948 --> 00:02:06.270
And I'm also joined by Nellie Petlick.

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:12.010
Nellie is a graduate
student here at Jackson.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.023
She spent four years as a Peace
Corps volunteer in Ukraine.

00:02:17.508 --> 00:02:19.510
And as we talk through things today,

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.290
I thought it was important
not just to think about this

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:24.840
from a historical and
geopolitical perspective,

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.483
but to also keep our eye
on the human perspective.

00:02:29.634 --> 00:02:31.563
And I hope Nellie might speak to that.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:37.420
The format today, I wanna
start with a question each

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:41.123
to Arne, to Tim, and then to Nellie.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.880
Possible some follow up,
and then Q&amp;A from you

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:49.940
because there's a lot of
expertise and curiosity

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.173
in the audience today.

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:54.233
I'll work my way down.

00:02:56.116 --> 00:02:57.993
Arne, let me start with you.

00:02:59.923 --> 00:03:02.680
You know, you've spent much of your career

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.423
thinking about great power competition.

00:03:07.423 --> 00:03:08.443
Wasn't always called that.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.560
Does the current situation
make sense to you

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.585
from that perspective?

00:03:15.585 --> 00:03:18.910
And with that sort of
geopolitical perspective,

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:20.760
how should we be thinking about this?

00:03:23.839 --> 00:03:26.626
- Well, so it depends
obviously on whose perspective

00:03:26.626 --> 00:03:28.321
you see this from, Jim.

00:03:28.321 --> 00:03:33.040
I mean, I think in Putin's own
mind, it sort of makes sense.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.720
I mean, what he is out to
do is to try to resurrect

00:03:38.242 --> 00:03:42.398
some form of Russian
empire, never mind communist,

00:03:42.398 --> 00:03:44.343
never mind the Soviet Union.

00:03:44.343 --> 00:03:48.290
I think looking at this otherwise
somewhat deranged speech

00:03:49.201 --> 00:03:51.221
that he gave at the
start of this conflict,

00:03:51.221 --> 00:03:53.346
you can actually understand quite a bit

00:03:53.346 --> 00:03:55.013
of what Putin thinks that he's after.

00:03:55.856 --> 00:04:00.370
And to him, it is about not
recognizing Ukrainian statehood,

00:04:01.358 --> 00:04:02.721
but more importantly,

00:04:02.721 --> 00:04:05.560
not recognizing Ukrainian
nationhood, right.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.650
And trying to force Ukrainians

00:04:09.618 --> 00:04:13.622
into this new Russian super
state that he wants to set up.

00:04:13.622 --> 00:04:18.456
So this is a war of conquest
of a kind that we rarely see,

00:04:18.456 --> 00:04:20.759
certainly towards the
end of the 20th century.

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:22.273
There are very few examples of this.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.999
You know, Iraq in Kuwait, perhaps.

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:29.600
So you try to take over
someone else's territory

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.650
and make it your own.

00:04:31.796 --> 00:04:33.778
On your question of whether it makes sense

00:04:33.778 --> 00:04:35.193
in a broader perspective,

00:04:36.699 --> 00:04:39.643
and even from a Russian
perspective beyond Putin,

00:04:39.643 --> 00:04:40.641
absolutely not.

00:04:40.641 --> 00:04:43.291
I mean, what this will lead
to is Russia's isolation.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:48.563
It will create a Russia that is even more,

00:04:49.484 --> 00:04:50.923
much more dependent on China,

00:04:51.806 --> 00:04:54.180
which is an angle I
hope we can cover here,

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.943
than what has been the case before.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.530
It will, of course also lead to tensions,

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.000
not just in Europe, but on a global scale

00:05:05.337 --> 00:05:07.282
that we haven't seen for a very long time

00:05:07.282 --> 00:05:09.325
because this war is in no way over.

00:05:09.325 --> 00:05:10.364
Let me underline that.

00:05:10.364 --> 00:05:12.024
I mean, that is really, really important,

00:05:12.024 --> 00:05:14.062
not just because the
Ukrainians are fighting

00:05:14.062 --> 00:05:15.400
for their country,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.170
but also because of the reactions

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:18.520
that you've seen elsewhere.

00:05:19.681 --> 00:05:21.480
I mean how this has brought people,

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.366
first and foremost in
Europe, but also elsewhere

00:05:24.366 --> 00:05:27.721
together to a degree that we
simply haven't seen before.

00:05:27.721 --> 00:05:30.763
And if there is another
sort of piece of information

00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:32.841
that people should look at,

00:05:32.841 --> 00:05:36.490
it is what happened in the
German Bundestag this past week,

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:39.770
which is a sea change
in terms of how Germany

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.635
is approaching its international affairs.

00:05:42.635 --> 00:05:44.420
Coming from a social democratic chancellor

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:46.917
and from a green foreign minister, right?

00:05:48.021 --> 00:05:50.436
So those are gonna, I
think, gonna be the effects.

00:05:50.436 --> 00:05:52.921
So for Russia as a whole,
for the international system

00:05:52.921 --> 00:05:54.470
makes no sense whatsoever,
but in Putin's own

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.920
increasingly deranged mind, you can see,

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.170
where this is coming from.

00:06:00.253 --> 00:06:02.830
- [Jim] Tim, what do you think?

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:07.690
I was, I wanna ask you sort of
from the Russian perspective,

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:12.690
you know, what's driving
this, does it make sense?

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.600
And then I'd love to hear your
reaction to what Arne said.

00:06:26.094 --> 00:06:29.240
- So first of all, I wanna
say, I just wanna acknowledge

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:33.340
the tremendous cognitive inequality

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.933
which this meeting represents.

00:06:35.933 --> 00:06:38.250
I wouldn't be here as
a historian of Ukraine

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.500
if it weren't for historians of Ukraine

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.310
who are now in Ukraine, of course,

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:46.310
unable to practice their craft
because they are refugees

00:06:47.272 --> 00:06:49.291
or they're in bunkers
or they're in basements

00:06:49.291 --> 00:06:50.929
or they're fighting as soldiers.

00:06:50.929 --> 00:06:53.349
So if it weren't for my
Ukrainian friends and colleagues

00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:55.492
from whom I've learned, if
it weren't for their books,

00:06:55.492 --> 00:06:57.270
if it weren't for their language,

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.591
I wouldn't be in a position,
I wouldn't have a job at Yale.

00:06:59.591 --> 00:07:01.595
I wouldn't be sitting here talking to you.

00:07:01.595 --> 00:07:03.074
So I just I wanna acknowledge that

00:07:03.074 --> 00:07:04.424
before I say anything else.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:08.260
And Jim, I hope we have a
second round of questions

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:09.810
before we go to the audience

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.510
because I wanna hear you ask
Arne specifically about China

00:07:14.358 --> 00:07:16.454
before we do anything else.

00:07:16.454 --> 00:07:17.773
That seems to me to be
incredibly important.

00:07:19.264 --> 00:07:21.574
So you ask about the Russian perspective,

00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:23.270
and as I'm sure, you know,

00:07:24.517 --> 00:07:27.745
one of the interesting
things about this war

00:07:27.745 --> 00:07:32.300
is that there isn't a Russian perspective.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:37.300
There isn't even the kind
of artificial euphoria

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.293
which was generated back in 2014.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:42.949
So as all of you know

00:07:42.949 --> 00:07:45.055
and many of you will intimately remember

00:07:45.055 --> 00:07:48.400
because I see people who
wrote about it and were there,

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.763
in 2014, Russia invaded
Ukraine the first time.

00:07:52.684 --> 00:07:55.910
In 2014, Russia managed
to occupy and annex Crimea

00:07:55.910 --> 00:08:00.910
and attempted with special
forces to provoke rebellions

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.357
in eight of the Ukrainian districts,

00:08:04.357 --> 00:08:06.760
eight of the Ukrainian
oblasts with partial success

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.693
in two of them.

00:08:08.693 --> 00:08:10.771
And that partial success had to do

00:08:10.771 --> 00:08:13.569
with the successful
provocation of an artillery war

00:08:13.569 --> 00:08:16.033
in and around the city of Donetsk in 2014.

00:08:17.154 --> 00:08:19.590
So I just remind you of
that because I'm trying to,

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:22.093
I'm trying to set up a certain contrast.

00:08:23.427 --> 00:08:27.168
Back then there was a
Russian propaganda build up

00:08:27.168 --> 00:08:28.209
to the war.

00:08:28.209 --> 00:08:32.830
So in 2013, there was a
sequence of propaganda

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:35.350
targeted for specific audiences.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:39.270
People on the right were
told that Ukraine was gay

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.845
and cosmopolitan, and
people on the far right

00:08:42.845 --> 00:08:44.611
were told that Ukraine was Nazi.

00:08:44.611 --> 00:08:47.120
And people on the far left
were told that Ukraine was,

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:48.390
sorry, have I got it confused.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:51.133
People on the far left
were told that people,

00:08:51.133 --> 00:08:52.331
that Ukraine was Nazi,

00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:54.953
there was a very coordinated
campaign which went on,

00:08:54.953 --> 00:08:56.850
and Russia basically won
that information war,

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.690
but more, but also the Russian public

00:09:00.973 --> 00:09:04.045
was prepared for something
to happen in Ukraine.

00:09:04.045 --> 00:09:06.140
And then when it did happen,
when Crimea was seized,

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:08.446
there was this moment of euphoria,

00:09:08.446 --> 00:09:10.795
a glorious speech in the
Russian parliament, you know,

00:09:10.795 --> 00:09:13.530
parliamentarians in tears, you
know, all that sort of thing.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:16.320
That we don't have this time around.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.650
There wasn't really a propaganda
preparation, you know,

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:22.573
for this war in Russia.

00:09:23.514 --> 00:09:25.950
And Putin's given a series of speeches

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:30.950
in which he seems almost
defensive about what he is doing.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:32.810
You know, he's even used the phrase,

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:35.885
like it might seem hard
for you to understand.

00:09:35.885 --> 00:09:38.592
You know, so when you get
to that point as a dictator,

00:09:38.592 --> 00:09:39.660
you know, and you're almost, you know,

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:42.160
you're in this didactic
or even hectoring mode

00:09:42.995 --> 00:09:44.475
with your own people,

00:09:44.475 --> 00:09:45.513
that's something different

00:09:45.513 --> 00:09:47.314
than being in the charismatic mode

00:09:47.314 --> 00:09:50.095
where everyone is going along
with you and carrying you

00:09:50.095 --> 00:09:51.238
and carrying you along.

00:09:51.238 --> 00:09:53.270
So, you know, not to pick on
the question, Jim, but I just,

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:55.960
I think it's important to note that Putin,

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.100
the relationship between
Putin and his own people

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:01.693
is quite different than
it was eight years ago.

00:10:01.693 --> 00:10:03.130
We don't know exactly what that means yet,

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:05.572
but we do know a couple of things.

00:10:05.572 --> 00:10:08.440
One of them is that
right from the beginning,

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.990
there were peace protests
in Russia, and every peace,

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.300
everyone who protests
for peace in Russia knows

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:17.793
that he or she is going to be
arrested almost immediately.

00:10:18.928 --> 00:10:19.840
So the people who actually protest

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:22.150
presumably represent a
very small percentage

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:25.280
of the people who sensed
that something was wrong.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:27.160
And the sensing that something is wrong

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:30.780
is very important in itself
because as you will all know,

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:34.650
the information is controlled
in Russia very effectively.

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:36.440
So the propaganda might not be working,

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:39.430
but the kind of knowledge
that we take for granted,

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:41.990
you know, on this campus
and in much of the world,

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:44.987
that kind of knowledge is not
easily available in Russia.

00:10:44.987 --> 00:10:47.180
So people who protested right
away were people who knew,

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:51.088
just knew that something was
off, that something was wrong.

00:10:51.088 --> 00:10:53.838
You know, this war should
not, should not be happening.

00:10:54.768 --> 00:10:57.450
The second thing that we
know is that this decision

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:00.490
was taken by a very small group of people.

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:01.920
I mean, by one man, of course,

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:03.580
but I think it was only known

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:06.103
to a very small group of people,

00:11:06.103 --> 00:11:08.450
which means that even the Russian elite

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.300
has not really had a
chance to adjust to this

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:12.070
or to find a way to rationalize it

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:14.170
or to make plans for their own future

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.950
under this regime of sanctions
which has now followed.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:21.160
So Russian points of
view are much more plural

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.700
than they were eight years ago.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:24.673
And then if,

00:11:25.667 --> 00:11:28.010
but if the question is
about how it makes sense

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.560
to Mr. Putin, I think Arne
Westad has already signaled

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:34.108
some important things.

00:11:34.108 --> 00:11:38.210
It depends, all of this
depends on the assumption

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:40.929
that there isn't really a Ukrainian nation

00:11:40.929 --> 00:11:42.200
and there isn't really a Ukrainian state.

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:45.567
I mean, as far, I mean,
Arne may have another idea,

00:11:45.567 --> 00:11:47.112
but I mean, as far as I can read it,

00:11:47.112 --> 00:11:48.970
the initial operational plan assumed

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:51.070
that the government would quickly collapse

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:53.811
and its members could
be captured and killed

00:11:53.811 --> 00:11:56.503
along with the entire
Ukrainian intellectual

00:11:56.503 --> 00:11:58.260
and civic and political elite.

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:00.970
The idea was that, you know,
Ukraine's not really a nation.

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:04.460
There's just a thin upper
crust of educated people

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.950
who have been somehow
artificially westernized.

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:09.670
So as soon as the tanks
roll in, they'll try to run,

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:11.370
we'll capture them, we'll kill them,

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:15.144
and then the Ukrainians are
a kind of inchoate mass.

00:12:15.144 --> 00:12:16.868
They're a kind of undefined group.

00:12:16.868 --> 00:12:17.930
They're a precolonial tribe.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:19.860
They'll just listen to
the stronger, right?

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:22.200
That I think is the governing assumption.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.080
That's a very familiar
governing assumption.

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:26.250
I mean, to any of those
of you who have studied

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:28.240
imperial history or colonial history,

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:30.270
you'll recognize that assumption

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.620
that the colonial power says

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:34.910
the other state is not really a state.

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:37.600
The colonial power says the other nation

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:39.920
is not really a nation, right?

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:41.870
And it's interesting to see
people in the, you know,

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:43.989
in the post-colonial world,

00:12:43.989 --> 00:12:46.949
like the Kenyan ambassador
for instance to the UN

00:12:46.949 --> 00:12:49.689
immediately recognize those patterns.

00:12:49.689 --> 00:12:51.710
But that is the fundamental assumption.

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:54.720
And it runs, you know, and as a historian,

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:58.000
it always kind of pains me to
even contest ideas like this

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:00.446
because they're not historical ideas.

00:13:00.446 --> 00:13:03.263
Like much as Putin pretends
to talk about history,

00:13:03.263 --> 00:13:04.220
these ideas don't come from history.

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:07.799
They come from colonial
reflexes basically.

00:13:07.799 --> 00:13:10.386
But so, but I have to contest them.

00:13:10.386 --> 00:13:12.340
I just have to make the,
point out the basic problem,

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:15.670
which is that, you know, no
nation and no state is perfect,

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:18.120
but the Ukrainian nation
and the Ukrainian state

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:19.640
are social and political realities

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:22.980
in so far as there are social
and political realities

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:24.445
in the world.

00:13:24.445 --> 00:13:26.160
Those things are not really contestable.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:29.940
And historically speaking,
what's odd is that

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:34.190
you don't even need to get to Maidan 2014

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:37.250
or Invasion 2022 to talk
about the Ukrainian nation.

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:38.100
I mean, it's true

00:13:39.004 --> 00:13:41.410
those things have consolidated
the Ukrainian nation,

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:45.030
or they've put the Ukrainian
nation before our eyes

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:47.660
and given us an opportunity to examine it.

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:50.890
But the idea that there's
a Ukrainian nation

00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:53.890
is just much older than that.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:56.570
I mean, the modern
Ukrainian National Movement

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:59.620
goes back 200 years.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.526
And before that there are
actually pre-modern elements

00:14:02.526 --> 00:14:03.920
of a Ukrainian nation

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.400
far before there are actually
elements of such a nation

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.646
in many other places.

00:14:07.646 --> 00:14:09.675
I mean, not to make the
point, you know, too strongly,

00:14:09.675 --> 00:14:11.391
but the history of Ukraine is much older

00:14:11.391 --> 00:14:13.081
than the history of the
United States of America.

00:14:13.081 --> 00:14:15.220
However you wish to calculate it.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:18.120
And so, you know, just looking
at it more practically,

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:21.282
when the Soviet Union was founded,

00:14:21.282 --> 00:14:23.810
it was founded in the form it was

00:14:24.661 --> 00:14:27.379
because people acknowledged
there was a Ukraine.

00:14:27.379 --> 00:14:30.344
You know, the so-called Russian
Civil War between, you know,

00:14:30.344 --> 00:14:32.080
let's say 1917 and 1921,

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:33.660
which was a very complicated affair

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:36.044
involving, you know, people supporting

00:14:36.044 --> 00:14:38.784
a Russian empire's revival,
people supporting a Ukraine,

00:14:38.784 --> 00:14:40.860
people supporting a Russian revolution,

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:44.080
that civil war was
largely fought in Ukraine.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.702
And it was fought by a
number of important people,

00:14:46.702 --> 00:14:48.399
some of, you know, some of the politics,

00:14:48.399 --> 00:14:50.281
politics was led by someone called Stalin.

00:14:50.281 --> 00:14:53.560
And those people recognized at the time

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:55.886
that there was a Ukraine.

00:14:55.886 --> 00:14:59.322
They knew they were fighting
against Ukrainian armies,

00:14:59.322 --> 00:15:01.483
right, at the time a hundred years ago.

00:15:02.341 --> 00:15:05.950
And the Soviet Union was
formed as a union of republics,

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:07.800
as opposed to some kind of, you know,

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:09.720
homogenous, revolutionary unit

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:14.250
because everyone knew at the
time there was a Ukraine.

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:15.900
If you look at the writings, you know,

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:18.601
I'll just like randomly pick some sources.

00:15:18.601 --> 00:15:19.930
But if you look at
League of Nations reports

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:22.844
or if you look at the
writings of Joseph Voltz

00:15:22.844 --> 00:15:24.661
or like whatever cultural or literary

00:15:24.661 --> 00:15:27.670
or statistical analysis, you
kind of prefer from the 1920s,

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:31.950
Ukraine as a category was
widely known to every one.

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:34.250
It wasn't some surprising thing, right?

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.259
And so its existence with,

00:15:36.259 --> 00:15:37.746
I'm not gonna do all
of Soviet history here.

00:15:37.746 --> 00:15:39.260
I'm just trying to make the
point that its existence

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:42.947
was already widely
acknowledged 100 years ago.

00:15:42.947 --> 00:15:44.410
And the fact that you would have to fight

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:47.200
to get control of it and
that this was dangerous,

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.710
this was known to the Bolsheviks.

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:52.362
It was known to Lenin and Stalin.

00:15:52.362 --> 00:15:53.820
And of course it was also
known to a later generation

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.070
of Soviet rulers, such as Khrushchev

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:59.020
who was sent to pacify Western Ukraine

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:00.710
at the end of the second World War.

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:03.520
The insurgency and counter
insurgency in Western Ukraine

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:04.820
after the second world war

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:06.970
required hundreds of thousands of deaths

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:10.544
and hundreds of thousands
of sentences to the Gulag

00:16:10.544 --> 00:16:11.803
for it to be brought down.

00:16:11.803 --> 00:16:12.950
So those are things that like basic

00:16:13.999 --> 00:16:15.432
that students of history would know.

00:16:15.432 --> 00:16:16.490
And what Mr. Putin has succeeded in doing

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:19.893
is forgetting Soviet history, right?

00:16:20.767 --> 00:16:22.845
Arne referred to a kind of Western,

00:16:22.845 --> 00:16:23.880
a kind of Russian empire.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:24.713
I mean,

00:16:25.724 --> 00:16:26.885
the kind of Russian empire he has in mind

00:16:26.885 --> 00:16:28.270
is a kind of mishmash of all of the things

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:29.750
that he likes from history.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:31.700
But what he has succeeded in driving away

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:33.530
is actual Soviet history

00:16:34.368 --> 00:16:36.510
and at least the practical recognition

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:39.240
that there was such a thing
as the Ukrainian nation,

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:41.065
which at a certain time everybody knew.

00:16:41.065 --> 00:16:43.730
What he's managed to do is to
take a kind of extreme version

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:47.127
of the 1970s when he was formed,

00:16:47.127 --> 00:16:49.742
a time of Russification
in this Soviet union.

00:16:49.742 --> 00:16:51.810
And then as he's aged in
the last decade especially,

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:55.660
he's taken on element of extreme
right Russian nationalism

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:58.440
and pushed these to this
kind of logical conclusion,

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.725
which he now seems to have settled on

00:17:00.725 --> 00:17:02.790
that there's no Ukrainian
nation and no Ukrainian state.

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:04.970
And of course, that's not true.

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:07.643
And now that it's been
proved not to be true,

00:17:07.643 --> 00:17:08.670
it seems like he has no choice,
but to try to, you know,

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:11.010
double down as one does
when one is a tyrant.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:13.040
And then that leads to the consequences

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.773
that Arne was speaking about.

00:17:17.587 --> 00:17:19.082
- Thank you, Tim.

00:17:19.082 --> 00:17:19.915
Nellie, What are you hearing

00:17:19.915 --> 00:17:21.140
from your friends and colleagues?

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.522
- Yeah, so just sort
of to add a little bit

00:17:25.522 --> 00:17:27.850
about my background since
I think most of you know

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:29.990
these two professors here.

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.270
So I was in Ukraine for
four years, as Jim said,

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:34.933
for the first two years
as a Peace Corps volunteer

00:17:34.933 --> 00:17:37.440
in a small town in South Central Ukraine,

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:40.094
where I had the
opportunity to meet Oleksii

00:17:40.094 --> 00:17:41.510
five years ago at a camp.

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:42.933
And then I decided to stay,

00:17:43.896 --> 00:17:45.170
and I started working for Education USA,

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:47.855
which is a network of the state department

00:17:47.855 --> 00:17:50.500
that promotes US higher
education opportunities.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:53.480
And so during that time for
about a year and a half,

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.900
I was doing outreach, and I
had the immense opportunity

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:59.870
to travel to 21 out of
24 regions of Ukraine

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:02.383
and really see the
diversity of the country.

00:18:04.039 --> 00:18:05.217
Can you hear me okay?

00:18:05.217 --> 00:18:06.050
Yes, okay, good.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:11.236
So I think, you know, as
a Peace Corps volunteer,

00:18:11.236 --> 00:18:13.204
I was in a town of 15,000 people.

00:18:13.204 --> 00:18:16.177
I was, there was another American there,

00:18:16.177 --> 00:18:17.616
but he was working on different projects

00:18:17.616 --> 00:18:18.940
in a different part of part o city.

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:20.880
So really I was, you know,

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:22.850
there was nothing special
about my position there.

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:26.076
I was integrating into the
community, learning the language,

00:18:26.076 --> 00:18:28.735
treated just as any other teacher.

00:18:28.735 --> 00:18:30.130
I was an English teacher at a high school.

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:33.661
So I was at all the staff
meetings and everything like that.

00:18:33.661 --> 00:18:35.950
You know, I wasn't treated any
differently as an American.

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:40.950
And so one thing that I
really want to highlight

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:45.362
is the generosity of the Ukrainian people.

00:18:45.362 --> 00:18:46.763
It's hard to even talk about.

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:50.020
It's gonna be a long hour guys, sorry.

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:54.780
In our training, when they
were sort of preparing us

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:55.613
to culturally adapt to Ukrainian life,

00:18:55.613 --> 00:18:58.370
they talked to us about how
Ukrainians have an outer circle

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:00.242
and an inner circle.

00:19:00.242 --> 00:19:03.460
And the outer circle can be
really hard to get through

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:05.950
because Ukrainians and other Slavic people

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:07.880
don't smile at you on
the street, you know,

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:09.937
like we do in America.

00:19:09.937 --> 00:19:11.580
And so it might seem
that they're very cold,

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:14.120
but we were told once you
get into that inner circle,

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:15.958
there's nothing else like it,

00:19:15.958 --> 00:19:17.141
and you're treated like family.

00:19:17.141 --> 00:19:18.673
And I found that to be incredibly true.

00:19:19.933 --> 00:19:21.280
And it was a generosity

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:26.280
that I really struggled to
understand and even to accept.

00:19:27.682 --> 00:19:30.300
I just didn't, I couldn't
really comprehend

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:32.580
how people could be so generous to someone

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:34.701
that they didn't even know,

00:19:34.701 --> 00:19:36.450
especially after all of the horrors

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:38.040
that they have been
through in their history,

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:41.617
as I'm sure Professor
Snyder will talk about.

00:19:41.617 --> 00:19:43.646
And I just couldn't believe
that they would be so welcoming

00:19:43.646 --> 00:19:45.300
to a stranger because Ukrainians
are the type of people

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:48.373
who give you everything they have,

00:19:48.373 --> 00:19:50.203
even when they have nothing left.

00:19:50.203 --> 00:19:52.300
And I think we're seeing that right now.

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:55.724
So I would just say sort of
from the human perspective,

00:19:55.724 --> 00:20:00.420
I'm really, really glad that
the world is seeing right now

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:04.890
the generosity and the love
and the fierceness to resist

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:06.924
of the Ukrainian people,

00:20:06.924 --> 00:20:11.410
and Putin truly, truly
messed with the wrong people.

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:13.980
And I think we're all seeing that.

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:16.860
And in terms of sort of
what I'm hearing and seeing,

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:18.459
Peace Corps volunteers,

00:20:18.459 --> 00:20:19.292
Ukraine was the biggest
Peace Corps country

00:20:19.292 --> 00:20:20.961
for many, many years.

00:20:20.961 --> 00:20:23.257
So returned Peace Corps
volunteers are now mobilizing.

00:20:23.257 --> 00:20:24.960
There's a network of about
1,100 of us right now,

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:28.298
working to help, of
course, friends and family

00:20:28.298 --> 00:20:29.337
that we still have there,

00:20:29.337 --> 00:20:30.973
arrange humanitarian
aid, things like that.

00:20:32.124 --> 00:20:34.770
But so much of that is already, of course,

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:36.721
already happening in Ukraine.

00:20:36.721 --> 00:20:38.503
I'm in touch with my community there.

00:20:38.503 --> 00:20:42.140
And I got a message from
a friend of mine there

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:43.930
about two days ago,
and she said, you know,

00:20:43.930 --> 00:20:46.021
in all the chaos and everything,

00:20:46.021 --> 00:20:47.370
people are abandoning their pets.

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:49.160
Is there any way that you can help out?

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:52.210
Get us some funds so we can
be buying food and shelters

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:53.870
for these animals.

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:55.780
And so that's what people there

00:20:56.766 --> 00:20:57.805
are thinking about right now.

00:20:57.805 --> 00:20:58.810
They're mobilizing to help people out.

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:02.420
You know, they're settling
IDPs in my town right now,

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:05.102
even though they themselves are at war.

00:21:05.102 --> 00:21:07.180
They're doing everything
they can to help people

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:08.521
who have fled from other
parts of the country.

00:21:08.521 --> 00:21:10.114
So I think that's just
what I wanna highlight

00:21:10.114 --> 00:21:11.090
is the generosity and the love.

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:13.670
And I'm just really glad
that the rest of the world

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:17.693
is seeing the spirit of
Ukrainians right now.

00:21:19.017 --> 00:21:20.017
- Thank you.

00:21:21.338 --> 00:21:23.660
Arne, I want to follow up a little bit

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:25.060
on a couple things you said.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:30.220
There was mention of
this leading to a Russia

00:21:31.436 --> 00:21:33.036
that is more dependent on China.

00:21:34.481 --> 00:21:37.423
You also spoke, I think the
phrase was tensions in Europe.

00:21:39.818 --> 00:21:43.320
It'd be great if you could
follow up on both of those.

00:21:44.275 --> 00:21:45.490
In terms of tensions in Europe,

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:49.203
my read is that this has
brought Europe more together,

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:52.570
which I don't, so I was puzzled

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:55.273
by the use of the phrase tensions.

00:21:55.273 --> 00:21:57.450
Maybe you could say a
little bit about that,

00:21:57.450 --> 00:22:00.383
and then moving forward

00:22:00.383 --> 00:22:03.240
with a Russia more dependent on China,

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:07.320
how does that change sort
of how we think about

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.913
the global balance of
power such that it is?

00:22:13.499 --> 00:22:16.259
- So let me talk about the
China angle to this first

00:22:16.259 --> 00:22:17.543
because it is really, really important,

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:21.733
and we don't know yet exactly
how it is going to play out.

00:22:23.678 --> 00:22:25.199
What's happening right at the moment

00:22:25.199 --> 00:22:27.079
is that the Chinese leaders

00:22:27.079 --> 00:22:30.540
are getting increasingly
embarrassed as they should be

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:32.540
with the behavior of their close partner

00:22:33.419 --> 00:22:35.783
in trying to destroy Ukraine.

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:41.480
What we need to do is to push even harder

00:22:43.078 --> 00:22:45.010
than what we have done
in order to increase

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:50.010
that sense of embarrassment,

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:55.440
of being hurt both directly and indirectly

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:57.090
by what their partners are doing.

00:22:58.919 --> 00:23:00.198
It's interesting that

00:23:00.198 --> 00:23:02.473
that was not the initial Chinese reaction.

00:23:02.473 --> 00:23:04.770
I mean, the initial Chinese
reaction was very clearly

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:07.720
that this was something that would work

00:23:07.720 --> 00:23:09.870
to China's advantage
if the Chinese leaders

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:13.160
were able to sit very
still and do very little,

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:16.360
and then the reactions,
the international reactions

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:18.330
against Russia's behavior

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:22.190
would force Russia even
more closely to China

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:23.823
to put it bluntly,

00:23:23.823 --> 00:23:27.515
to be exploited more efficiently by China,

00:23:27.515 --> 00:23:29.617
the way Russia is already today.

00:23:29.617 --> 00:23:30.680
One of the big issues

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:33.300
that you should tell all
of your Russian friends

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:36.670
if you have any, is that Putin's strategy

00:23:36.670 --> 00:23:40.140
has led to an almost complete
loss of Russian sovereignty

00:23:41.279 --> 00:23:43.670
with regard to China who was
taking advantage of this,

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:46.683
even before the invasion

00:23:46.683 --> 00:23:49.434
and attempted occupation
of Ukraine took place,

00:23:49.434 --> 00:23:52.453
but now seem to be having
an even better opportunity.

00:23:53.352 --> 00:23:55.070
The problem, however, for
the Chinese leadership,

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:58.320
is that what we see now
over television screens

00:23:59.709 --> 00:24:03.110
of the behavior that Russian
troops are showing in Ukraine

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:06.270
goes against so much
of what they themselves

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:10.727
have tried to propagate to
the world of Chinese aims.

00:24:10.727 --> 00:24:14.040
Ever since the People's Republic
of China was set up in 1949

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:18.010
on the principles of sovereignty,
territorial integrity,

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:20.480
non-intervention, you know,

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:22.640
which the Chinese have been going on about

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:23.760
for a very long time.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:27.387
So these are the buttons
that we should try to hit.

00:24:27.387 --> 00:24:29.108
And I've been trying to hit as well

00:24:29.108 --> 00:24:31.866
with the Chinese when I speak to people

00:24:31.866 --> 00:24:33.523
in news outlets in China about this.

00:24:34.948 --> 00:24:39.113
And I think that sense
that this is problematic,

00:24:39.113 --> 00:24:41.704
including to explain to their own people

00:24:41.704 --> 00:24:46.346
what is going on is now
increasing in China.

00:24:46.346 --> 00:24:48.511
It was very interesting to see today

00:24:48.511 --> 00:24:50.790
that they're also starting to prepare

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:52.303
for an economic backlash.

00:24:53.388 --> 00:24:55.370
The Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank,

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:58.650
which is in my view
entirely dominated by China

00:24:58.650 --> 00:25:01.780
has now stopped issuing loans to Russia.

00:25:01.780 --> 00:25:04.590
And the reason why they've done that

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:07.546
is not because the Chinese are moving away

00:25:07.546 --> 00:25:08.560
from the Russians diplomatically,

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:11.384
but because of the fear of the effect

00:25:11.384 --> 00:25:13.363
of US and Western secondary sanctions,

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:17.466
which could hit that bank,

00:25:17.466 --> 00:25:19.050
will hit that bank if they continue

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:21.003
their current lending activities.

00:25:22.232 --> 00:25:23.150
So these are the kinds of things

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:25.190
that we ought to think about.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:29.169
I think it is important
in the kind of situation

00:25:29.169 --> 00:25:31.433
that we have now to continue
to speak to the Chinese

00:25:31.433 --> 00:25:33.410
about this, even though the answers we get

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:36.723
are not the answers that
we would like to have,

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:40.460
but China is going to be
important with regard to this.

00:25:40.460 --> 00:25:43.593
I also think speaking
of the popular level,

00:25:44.646 --> 00:25:47.251
I think among ordinary people in China,

00:25:47.251 --> 00:25:49.601
in spite of the heavily
censored Chinese media,

00:25:50.648 --> 00:25:54.230
there is an increasing concern
about what is happening.

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:55.366
- [Jim] I was gonna ask you about that

00:25:55.366 --> 00:25:58.084
because I know you stay in
touch with colleagues there,

00:25:58.084 --> 00:25:59.189
speak the language.

00:25:59.189 --> 00:26:01.230
- Yeah, I think there is
absolutely no doubt about that.

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:03.308
And it is interesting.

00:26:03.308 --> 00:26:04.313
It's interesting,

00:26:04.313 --> 00:26:06.440
particularly over the
last three to four days

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:08.453
to see how that has increased,

00:26:09.514 --> 00:26:11.685
and that's obviously also
something by, you know,

00:26:11.685 --> 00:26:15.209
by publishing in Chinese because
Chinese are smart people.

00:26:15.209 --> 00:26:18.930
They find a way around the great firewall

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:21.602
that their regime has
set up to prevent them

00:26:21.602 --> 00:26:22.768
from getting information.

00:26:22.768 --> 00:26:24.369
So we should do more on that.

00:26:24.369 --> 00:26:26.360
Just quickly, Jim, on Europe.

00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:27.960
So when I used the term tension,

00:26:28.909 --> 00:26:32.188
I meant, first and foremost,
in terms of the relationship

00:26:32.188 --> 00:26:34.560
between Russia and the rest of Europe.

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:39.189
That's where, you know,
for a very long time,

00:26:39.189 --> 00:26:43.206
very clearly there is going
to be increased tension.

00:26:43.206 --> 00:26:45.827
It's true that this has
brought Europe together

00:26:45.827 --> 00:26:47.670
to a remarkable degree, but
the question still remains,

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:50.370
and we have to be able to ask ourselves

00:26:50.370 --> 00:26:54.449
these tough questions, what should Europe,

00:26:54.449 --> 00:26:56.833
which is really at the front
line of this conflict, do?

00:26:57.893 --> 00:27:00.488
You know, with regard to
the fighting that goes on

00:27:00.488 --> 00:27:01.430
at the moment, and on that as well,

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:03.783
there are different answers.

00:27:04.691 --> 00:27:09.000
I am really struck by the degree
to which European neutrals

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:11.230
like Finland and Sweden

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:15.000
have been willing to try to
support Ukraine militarily

00:27:16.227 --> 00:27:19.191
in a way that again, only a few weeks ago,

00:27:19.191 --> 00:27:21.713
no one would have dreamt
about being possible.

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.568
And we're supplying them with weapons

00:27:25.568 --> 00:27:26.988
that they actually need

00:27:26.988 --> 00:27:27.821
because these are the kinds of weapons

00:27:27.821 --> 00:27:30.323
that they have developed
for their own defense,

00:27:30.323 --> 00:27:33.193
obviously, thinking ahead about Russia.

00:27:34.406 --> 00:27:36.107
I think that's hugely important

00:27:36.107 --> 00:27:37.750
because it's gonna spill over

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:38.900
to other European countries as well.

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:42.771
In what to me is the crucial willingness

00:27:42.771 --> 00:27:47.500
to resupply Ukrainians who
are fighting for their lives

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:49.650
against this invasion.

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:50.483
I want to underline that.

00:27:50.483 --> 00:27:52.790
So there are some people in Europe

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:55.467
and there are some people
here who are saying,

00:27:55.467 --> 00:27:56.848
we have to be careful with this

00:27:56.848 --> 00:27:59.548
because we do not want to
unleash the third world war.

00:28:00.627 --> 00:28:05.627
Now with regard to resupplying
the Ukrainians on the ground

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:09.230
with the kind of weapons
that they actually need

00:28:09.230 --> 00:28:12.406
at the moment that is not
risking a third world war.

00:28:12.406 --> 00:28:14.730
That is making the risk
of a third world war

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:17.890
significantly lower than
what it would've been

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:22.850
not doing it because it's only
if Putin's regime realizes,

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:27.040
that they get kind of resistance
that we are now seeing

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:30.660
in Ukraine that it will
be possible to push back

00:28:31.626 --> 00:28:32.776
in later cases as well.

00:28:33.709 --> 00:28:34.748
If we don't do that,

00:28:34.748 --> 00:28:36.725
the situation would be
much more dangerous.

00:28:36.725 --> 00:28:39.433
So I think it's important to underline

00:28:39.433 --> 00:28:40.751
that aspect of it as well.

00:28:40.751 --> 00:28:43.028
I was up at, in a prearranged visit

00:28:43.028 --> 00:28:43.861
up at West Point yesterday

00:28:44.769 --> 00:28:47.352
and spoke to people there about it.

00:28:47.352 --> 00:28:49.090
And as many of you will know,

00:28:49.090 --> 00:28:52.500
military people tend to be the
most reluctant that we have

00:28:53.354 --> 00:28:54.840
to think about actually getting involved

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:55.840
in foreign conflict.

00:28:56.688 --> 00:28:59.711
But I wanted to get across
to them that, you know,

00:28:59.711 --> 00:29:02.450
resupplying Ukraine in any way that we can

00:29:03.391 --> 00:29:04.582
is actually for the,

00:29:04.582 --> 00:29:06.887
in a strategic sense, for
the good of this country

00:29:06.887 --> 00:29:07.720
and for good of it.

00:29:09.765 --> 00:29:12.115
- [Jim] Tim, do you wanna
add anything to that?

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:21.632
- I wanted to say that
I very much appreciated

00:29:21.632 --> 00:29:25.493
Nellie's remarks about
her time in Ukraine.

00:29:26.390 --> 00:29:30.818
It's, you know, insofar as
we have anything to say,

00:29:30.818 --> 00:29:32.269
you know, it's because we've at some point

00:29:32.269 --> 00:29:34.707
had someone to talk to
and someone to learn from.

00:29:34.707 --> 00:29:35.773
So I very much appreciated that.

00:29:36.727 --> 00:29:39.021
I'll just add a maybe couple brief words

00:29:39.021 --> 00:29:41.883
about Europe and about Russia.

00:29:43.508 --> 00:29:45.308
What I wanted, I mean, going back to

00:29:46.520 --> 00:29:49.551
your initial question, Jim,
one thing that I wanted to say,

00:29:49.551 --> 00:29:52.510
which I forgot to say, is
that even in the Soviet Union,

00:29:52.510 --> 00:29:54.633
Ukraine was governed as Ukraine.

00:29:55.907 --> 00:30:00.303
So it was governed at times
with horrible repressiveness,

00:30:01.184 --> 00:30:03.470
as for example, during
the first five year plan,

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:07.440
which led to political decisions in Moscow

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:12.144
to allow about 4 million
Ukrainians to die in a famine

00:30:12.144 --> 00:30:13.133
who did not have to die.

00:30:14.506 --> 00:30:17.711
And that by the way goes
to the question another way

00:30:17.711 --> 00:30:20.900
because when you ask, you
know, what is Putin thinking?

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:23.547
He's thinking that none of that is true.

00:30:23.547 --> 00:30:26.780
You know, whereas for
Ukrainians, you know,

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:28.980
regardless of whether
the Holodomor comes up

00:30:29.982 --> 00:30:31.468
in political conversations or not,

00:30:31.468 --> 00:30:33.021
it's a lived family reality.

00:30:33.021 --> 00:30:34.020
It's a matter of, you know, grandparents,

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:36.646
great-grandparents, people know it's true,

00:30:36.646 --> 00:30:38.289
whether you talk about it or not,

00:30:38.289 --> 00:30:40.013
whether it comes up in
international politics or not.

00:30:40.907 --> 00:30:42.451
And that, you know, that interpretation

00:30:42.451 --> 00:30:46.105
of the Stalin years is one of the things

00:30:46.105 --> 00:30:48.370
which marks off Ukrainians from Russians.

00:30:48.370 --> 00:30:50.944
You know, people, when
it comes to identity,

00:30:50.944 --> 00:30:53.008
everybody wants to make
like these quick jumps,

00:30:53.008 --> 00:30:54.440
like it should be about
language, and, you know,

00:30:54.440 --> 00:30:56.670
that's like a shortcut,
and it's completely wrong.

00:30:56.670 --> 00:30:59.007
And I wish people wouldn't do it.

00:30:59.007 --> 00:31:00.702
You know, I mean Arne speaks Mandarin,

00:31:00.702 --> 00:31:03.413
but that doesn't, you
know. (Arne laughing)

00:31:04.321 --> 00:31:06.219
You know, right?

00:31:06.219 --> 00:31:07.790
- Though, I have had people, Tim, in China

00:31:07.790 --> 00:31:08.800
asking me which part of China I was from.

00:31:08.800 --> 00:31:11.763
- That's good.
- That's never stopped.

00:31:11.763 --> 00:31:13.404
- That's good.

00:31:13.404 --> 00:31:14.585
- Stopped now, I think.

00:31:14.585 --> 00:31:15.418
- That's good.

00:31:15.418 --> 00:31:16.280
That's as good as it gets with languages

00:31:16.280 --> 00:31:18.097
when people like know, they
think you're from their country,

00:31:18.097 --> 00:31:19.063
but they're not sure which part.

00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:22.781
That's about as good as it
gets, but you see my point.

00:31:22.781 --> 00:31:24.964
I mean, we're all speaking English here,

00:31:24.964 --> 00:31:26.330
but I mean, there may not
be anybody in this room

00:31:26.330 --> 00:31:29.683
who's from the United
Kingdom, and if, you know,

00:31:29.683 --> 00:31:32.644
and if you are, you're
probably too shy to say so,

00:31:32.644 --> 00:31:36.382
but so, but people want
there to be a shortcut.

00:31:36.382 --> 00:31:37.630
They wanna say like, I wanna
be able to say who you are

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:40.860
because of how you look
or, you know, what you say,

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:44.440
but identity is actually about experience,

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:46.483
about, you know, biographical experience,

00:31:46.483 --> 00:31:48.521
your own experience, the
experience of your family,

00:31:48.521 --> 00:31:50.696
the experience that you
think you understand,

00:31:50.696 --> 00:31:52.896
and the experiences of
Ukrainians, you know,

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:56.439
not just in the last decade,
not just in the last few days,

00:31:56.439 --> 00:31:59.202
you know, but in the last
few years with Maidan,

00:31:59.202 --> 00:32:01.050
in the last 30 years with
a generation of people

00:32:01.050 --> 00:32:05.180
who have grown up in what is a admittedly,

00:32:05.180 --> 00:32:07.810
a sometimes cranky and
ramshackle and unpredictable,

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:10.380
but nevertheless, independent state.

00:32:10.380 --> 00:32:12.810
You know, people who are,

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:15.850
people who are, you know, 45 and under now

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:18.730
have had the dominant
experiences of their life

00:32:18.730 --> 00:32:20.760
in an independent Ukraine,

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:23.400
but it goes back much further than that.

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:26.659
It goes back, the differences
go back hundreds of years.

00:32:26.659 --> 00:32:27.620
And I won't bore you with them

00:32:27.620 --> 00:32:29.703
because that's what I work on.

00:32:29.703 --> 00:32:31.742
And you should always know
that what you yourself work on

00:32:31.742 --> 00:32:34.667
is gonna be boring to an
audience, so I won't dwell on it.

00:32:34.667 --> 00:32:36.617
But the point though, the point though,

00:32:37.565 --> 00:32:39.104
is that when you wanna see the difference

00:32:39.104 --> 00:32:40.880
between Russians and
Ukrainians, it's often,

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:43.550
like it's a matter of experience.

00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:46.490
And the Stalin period is something that,

00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:50.644
what Putin is trying to
literally like, write it out

00:32:50.644 --> 00:32:51.764
and flatten it out.

00:32:51.764 --> 00:32:53.883
I don't know how many of you pay attention

00:32:53.883 --> 00:32:56.884
to these memory laws, but
there's a law in Russia

00:32:56.884 --> 00:32:58.585
which says that you're not
allowed to speak about the period

00:32:58.585 --> 00:32:59.750
1939 to 1941.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:02.380
You're quite, and then
right before the invasion,

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:05.780
the criminal penalties
on that particular law

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:08.910
were strengthened and not accidentally

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:10.520
because Putin's behavior

00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:13.980
is very much like
Stalin's behavior in 1939.

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:14.910
He's going west.

00:33:14.910 --> 00:33:17.800
He's claiming that it's to
rescue endangered compatriots.

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:19.975
He's claiming that he's doing it

00:33:19.975 --> 00:33:21.693
because the state to his immediate west

00:33:21.693 --> 00:33:24.170
no longer really exists or doesn't exist.

00:33:24.170 --> 00:33:27.952
That's pretty much exactly
what Stalin said in 1939

00:33:27.952 --> 00:33:29.850
when the Soviet Union invaded Poland.

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:32.208
But my deeper point here is that

00:33:32.208 --> 00:33:35.070
what the Russian leadership
is doing is flattening out

00:33:35.917 --> 00:33:38.600
a history which is central to the memory,

00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:41.195
the lived memory, not a political memory

00:33:41.195 --> 00:33:42.613
of people in Ukraine.

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:47.817
And so that's one thing
that I wanted to get to

00:33:47.817 --> 00:33:50.936
that there is this Soviet,
there's this Soviet experience,

00:33:50.936 --> 00:33:53.954
which was very different for
Ukrainians and for Russians

00:33:53.954 --> 00:33:55.433
and is remembered as such,

00:33:56.299 --> 00:33:59.300
but that even in the Soviet Union,

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:02.674
no one was denying that
there was a Ukraine.

00:34:02.674 --> 00:34:05.255
Like it was governed as a,
it was governed as a Ukraine.

00:34:05.255 --> 00:34:06.230
And that's what, as Arne was saying,

00:34:06.230 --> 00:34:10.000
that's what makes this proposal
to govern Ukraine as Russia

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:11.790
so radical.

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:14.670
That's never been done ever.

00:34:14.670 --> 00:34:18.474
The Russian empire was not
a Russian national state.

00:34:18.474 --> 00:34:19.920
It didn't operate according
to national principles.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:21.953
The people who governed it,

00:34:21.953 --> 00:34:23.730
not to put too fine a point
on it, were not Russians.

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:26.073
The elite in the Russian empire

00:34:26.073 --> 00:34:28.697
was not ethnically
Russian for the most part.

00:34:28.697 --> 00:34:30.551
It was, oh, you look surprised.

00:34:30.551 --> 00:34:31.913
It was German and Swedish.

00:34:31.913 --> 00:34:33.060
You know, it was Polish.

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:36.812
You know, when the Russian
empire took over Kiev

00:34:36.812 --> 00:34:39.600
in the late 17th century,
that was the only institution

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:42.234
of higher education that they had.

00:34:42.234 --> 00:34:44.250
So, I mean, there were no,
there was no publishing.

00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:46.612
There was no university
in Russia at the time.

00:34:46.612 --> 00:34:48.120
But so what I'm trying to say is that like

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:51.920
this notion of Russia
governing Ukraine as Russia

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:54.433
is entirely anachronistic and novel.

00:34:55.267 --> 00:34:56.829
It's never happened before.

00:34:56.829 --> 00:34:59.127
And so Putin like reaching back

00:34:59.127 --> 00:35:00.411
and talking about a thousand years

00:35:00.411 --> 00:35:01.977
and all this total nonsense,

00:35:01.977 --> 00:35:04.000
Russia wasn't a modern nation then, right?

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:06.830
The Russian empire controlled
much of what is now Ukraine,

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:09.850
but it wasn't governing
it as a national state.

00:35:09.850 --> 00:35:12.530
The Soviet Union controlled
what is now Ukraine,

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:14.640
but it wasn't governing
it as a national state.

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:16.780
The idea that a Russian,

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:20.300
a Russia on ethnic principles
can dominate Ukraine

00:35:20.300 --> 00:35:22.760
is a really radical idea.

00:35:22.760 --> 00:35:25.990
It's the most radical version
of how to govern Ukraine

00:35:25.990 --> 00:35:28.830
that's ever been around
except for the Nazi one.

00:35:28.830 --> 00:35:31.513
And it's coming at a point in history

00:35:31.513 --> 00:35:34.996
where the Ukrainian nation
has been most fully formed.

00:35:34.996 --> 00:35:36.160
So I just, I wanted to
bring that particular point

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:39.501
like that to a sharp point.

00:35:39.501 --> 00:35:40.790
Thanks.

00:35:40.790 --> 00:35:42.203
- Yeah, one last question.

00:35:43.123 --> 00:35:46.250
And it's not a very fair
one before we open it up.

00:35:46.250 --> 00:35:48.800
You know, Arne, you said
it's important to realize

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:50.593
this is not close to being over.

00:35:52.855 --> 00:35:53.733
It's an important point.

00:35:54.675 --> 00:35:56.440
I'm wondering if either you or Tim

00:35:56.440 --> 00:36:01.350
would to be willing to
hazard a guesstimate,

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:03.980
a prediction as to how this,

00:36:03.980 --> 00:36:06.780
I don't wanna say ends
because it will be ongoing,

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:09.963
but how this plays out over
the next six to 12 months.

00:36:12.839 --> 00:36:15.020
- I think that goes to
you first. (Arne laughing)

00:36:15.020 --> 00:36:17.470
- And as I said, I know
it's not a fair question.

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:23.694
- Well, you also get a slightly
unfair answer to it, Jim.

00:36:23.694 --> 00:36:26.010
It won't end until the Russian troops

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:27.653
have withdrawn from Ukraine.

00:36:28.698 --> 00:36:30.198
I mean, that's the key element

00:36:31.118 --> 00:36:33.359
that it's important to recognize now.

00:36:33.359 --> 00:36:35.630
I mean, the idea that it's possible

00:36:35.630 --> 00:36:38.194
to get any kind of settlement

00:36:38.194 --> 00:36:43.194
that will lead to any form of stability,

00:36:43.771 --> 00:36:46.757
even the kind of stability
that Putin wants to impose

00:36:46.757 --> 00:36:49.210
in Ukraine without a
withdrawal of Russian troops

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:51.391
is unimaginable.

00:36:51.391 --> 00:36:53.330
And we are seeing that now
every day on the streets

00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:54.697
of Ukrainian cities.

00:36:56.597 --> 00:36:59.420
The big question is of
course how long it will take

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:04.420
before the Russians in charge
realize that that is the case.

00:37:05.078 --> 00:37:06.793
And the only answer I have to that

00:37:06.793 --> 00:37:08.510
is that that depends on
the degree of pressure,

00:37:08.510 --> 00:37:10.813
not just the pressure they get

00:37:10.813 --> 00:37:15.280
by a lot of young Russian
people coming back to Russia

00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:18.740
in coffins to put it bluntly,
but also the kind of pressure

00:37:18.740 --> 00:37:21.450
that we can put on Russia.

00:37:21.450 --> 00:37:25.080
So as people up here know,

00:37:25.080 --> 00:37:30.080
I've worked very closely
with colleagues in Russia

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:33.679
for quite some time as an historian

00:37:33.679 --> 00:37:35.232
because parts of my interest

00:37:35.232 --> 00:37:37.132
have been connected to Soviet history.

00:37:38.271 --> 00:37:42.880
And of course, I try to
make use of that opportunity

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:43.760
to contact people

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:46.180
while as I was saying
to Tim the other day,

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:50.856
while I was resigning from the
various boards and committees

00:37:50.856 --> 00:37:53.320
and various other things that
I found myself on in Russia

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:58.030
also to contact friends and colleagues

00:37:58.030 --> 00:38:01.350
and acquaintances directly and tell them

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:02.370
both why I'd done that,

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:06.190
but also what I think the
consequences for Russia will be

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:08.390
of what they have now done.

00:38:09.288 --> 00:38:12.143
So I think that's in a
way the timeline here.

00:38:14.510 --> 00:38:17.640
So this is also where
China is important, right?

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:19.875
Let me just comment on that very briefly

00:38:19.875 --> 00:38:21.330
if I can, Jim, again, because, you know,

00:38:21.330 --> 00:38:23.390
this is the first global crisis

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:26.960
where I think that China
potentially has more influence

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:28.660
than this country.

00:38:28.660 --> 00:38:30.371
- [Tim] Interesting.

00:38:30.371 --> 00:38:32.890
- And so what I and
others are trying to tell

00:38:32.890 --> 00:38:35.978
our Chinese friends is
that with great power

00:38:35.978 --> 00:38:37.493
comes great responsibility.

00:38:37.493 --> 00:38:39.440
You can't simply sit back and do nothing

00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:42.253
and hope that the whole
thing will blow over

00:38:42.253 --> 00:38:43.190
in a way that will serve you.

00:38:43.190 --> 00:38:44.970
You can't do it for domestic reasons,

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:48.170
and you can't do it for
international reasons.

00:38:48.170 --> 00:38:51.750
And here some parallels,
imprecise as they might be

00:38:52.673 --> 00:38:56.020
from history, but concerning as they are,

00:38:56.020 --> 00:38:58.296
need to be put to them.

00:38:58.296 --> 00:38:59.520
So one of the perspectives that I now put

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:03.220
to my Chinese friends is the parallel

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:05.380
which I've been discussing
in other context

00:39:05.380 --> 00:39:08.890
with the kind of global
situation that we see now

00:39:08.890 --> 00:39:12.000
and the situation in Europe
at the end of the 19th

00:39:12.988 --> 00:39:14.607
and the beginning of
the 20th century, right?

00:39:15.588 --> 00:39:18.630
Where you had this tremendous
growth in German power.

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:20.490
Germany had just been reunified.

00:39:20.490 --> 00:39:21.720
It was industrializing.

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:24.040
It was at the forefront of technology.

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:26.480
It was obviously moving towards

00:39:26.480 --> 00:39:29.753
and aiming towards a
dominant position in Europe.

00:39:31.484 --> 00:39:35.340
Right next to Germany was a ancient

00:39:35.340 --> 00:39:38.853
but rather decrepit
empire of Austria Hungary,

00:39:39.857 --> 00:39:42.400
which had problems with
a lot of nationalities

00:39:42.400 --> 00:39:46.880
inside the empire, but also
along its borders, right?

00:39:46.880 --> 00:39:51.140
And for reasons that were
strategic in a very limited sense,

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:56.140
Germany increasingly became
a close ally of that empire.

00:39:56.870 --> 00:39:59.963
Even if the Germans knew that
this would lead to trouble.

00:40:00.979 --> 00:40:02.480
This, you know, there
would be difficulties

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:03.313
coming out of that.

00:40:04.421 --> 00:40:06.210
They couldn't foresee what
happened in the summer of 1914,

00:40:06.210 --> 00:40:08.781
but there clearly were difficulties.

00:40:08.781 --> 00:40:09.913
This is an imprecise,

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:12.902
but still rather instructive parallel

00:40:12.902 --> 00:40:16.063
to the relationship between
China and Russia today.

00:40:16.063 --> 00:40:17.800
And it should really be a memento

00:40:18.881 --> 00:40:21.505
for the Chinese leaders
to think about this,

00:40:21.505 --> 00:40:22.810
that they could end up
in a kind of situation

00:40:22.810 --> 00:40:24.690
because of what the Russians are doing

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:29.121
that would really move
China away from the path

00:40:29.121 --> 00:40:31.210
that it's on now to even
within its own region

00:40:31.210 --> 00:40:33.270
towards further, further influence

00:40:33.270 --> 00:40:36.290
for reasons that are not
primarily of Chinese doing

00:40:36.290 --> 00:40:38.717
because they are sitting back on this,

00:40:38.717 --> 00:40:40.863
but because of their
pseudo-alliance with the Russians.

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:43.660
- [Jim] Tim, do you wanna?

00:40:44.522 --> 00:40:46.440
- Yeah, let me have a crack at that

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:48.950
from a different, from a different angle.

00:40:48.950 --> 00:40:51.640
I mean, from a great distance, Arne,

00:40:51.640 --> 00:40:53.660
I'm also struck by this sort of triangle

00:40:53.660 --> 00:40:58.660
of US, China, Russia,
where, it's the Chinese,

00:40:59.710 --> 00:41:02.810
it seems to me could turn
this off in a heartbeat

00:41:02.810 --> 00:41:03.963
if they wanted to,

00:41:05.199 --> 00:41:08.290
but their first reaction is to
put the responsibility on us,

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:10.060
and we actually have much less ability

00:41:10.060 --> 00:41:13.379
to do anything about
it one way or another.

00:41:13.379 --> 00:41:16.763
There's something very striking
about that juxtaposition,

00:41:16.763 --> 00:41:18.476
but on how this comes to an end.

00:41:18.476 --> 00:41:20.583
I just wanna stress one dimension of it,

00:41:20.583 --> 00:41:21.690
which we haven't really talked about,

00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:24.293
which is the dimension of unreality,

00:41:25.302 --> 00:41:26.710
the dimension of
propaganda or information,

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:29.500
the dimension of how people
think about all of this,

00:41:29.500 --> 00:41:32.402
because, while, of course, you know,

00:41:32.402 --> 00:41:34.904
the war in Ukraine is
very realm and, you know,

00:41:34.904 --> 00:41:38.421
people are killing and dying
now, you know, and living.

00:41:38.421 --> 00:41:43.320
I mean, a treasured
colleague, incredible person

00:41:43.320 --> 00:41:46.460
just got married in her bulletproof vest

00:41:46.460 --> 00:41:47.823
before this thing started.

00:41:49.390 --> 00:41:51.970
Nevertheless, it's also very important

00:41:54.281 --> 00:41:57.299
to ask whether reality, you know,

00:41:57.299 --> 00:41:58.797
I'm very old fashioned about these things,

00:41:58.797 --> 00:42:00.740
I believe in facts and truth and so on.

00:42:00.740 --> 00:42:03.563
Whether reality defends
itself against unreality,

00:42:04.737 --> 00:42:07.370
because what's been
characteristic of 21st century war

00:42:07.370 --> 00:42:11.390
in Russia has been that
the informational character

00:42:11.390 --> 00:42:15.881
has run out way ahead of
the kinetic character.

00:42:15.881 --> 00:42:18.570
So they've done much
better in the war for minds

00:42:19.405 --> 00:42:22.163
than they've done in the war
for territory in general.

00:42:23.021 --> 00:42:25.982
And this is a reflection
of an interesting turn

00:42:25.982 --> 00:42:28.700
that was taken in Russian
politics in society

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:30.604
in the early 21st century.

00:42:30.604 --> 00:42:32.840
Peter Pomerantsev has a
really good book about this

00:42:32.840 --> 00:42:35.667
called "Nothing is True,
and Everything is Possible,"

00:42:36.558 --> 00:42:39.163
where, you know, when
certain conditions prevail,

00:42:40.361 --> 00:42:43.061
extreme inequality of wealth,
centralization of media,

00:42:43.914 --> 00:42:46.540
you know, major oligarchical
clan is in charge of the state.

00:42:46.540 --> 00:42:50.600
You can govern not by
promising people policy

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:52.763
based on facts that changes the future,

00:42:53.771 --> 00:42:56.230
but you can govern by
having the best spectacle

00:42:56.230 --> 00:42:58.560
and by crowding out other spectacles

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:00.160
or other sources of information.

00:43:01.172 --> 00:43:03.889
And so Russia then has been
fighting wars that way.

00:43:03.889 --> 00:43:05.868
And the war in 2014 against Ukraine

00:43:05.868 --> 00:43:07.290
was a success in that way.

00:43:07.290 --> 00:43:10.084
It wasn't actually a battlefield
success particularly.

00:43:10.084 --> 00:43:11.550
It went less well for
them than they expected

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:12.523
on the battlefield,

00:43:13.444 --> 00:43:15.890
but they did a very good job
with Western public opinion.

00:43:15.890 --> 00:43:19.200
And they did a very good job
with American public opinion.

00:43:19.200 --> 00:43:21.880
They managed to prevent
American and European elites

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:24.610
from seeing the basic things
that were happening, right?

00:43:24.610 --> 00:43:25.830
It's still hard.

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:27.620
People still feel a kind
of internal resistance

00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:30.869
to saying Russia invaded Ukraine in 2014,

00:43:30.869 --> 00:43:31.915
although that's the most,

00:43:31.915 --> 00:43:33.889
you know, that's the
simplest most lapidarian,

00:43:33.889 --> 00:43:37.070
most accurate characterization
of what happened.

00:43:37.070 --> 00:43:39.685
There are all kinds of
pre-programmed reflexes

00:43:39.685 --> 00:43:40.750
that people have instead of that,

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:42.910
even though that's, you know,

00:43:42.910 --> 00:43:44.512
that's the most important
thing that happened.

00:43:44.512 --> 00:43:47.432
For this war, it has gone
less well for Russia.

00:43:47.432 --> 00:43:50.020
People learned a lot in
the last eight years.

00:43:50.020 --> 00:43:52.610
Ukrainians learned a lot.

00:43:52.610 --> 00:43:56.260
It's, the Ukrainians
have done a very good job

00:43:56.260 --> 00:43:58.713
at expressing themselves as themselves.

00:43:59.791 --> 00:44:00.650
So not trying to take up the cudgels

00:44:00.650 --> 00:44:02.420
and fight a Russian style information war,

00:44:02.420 --> 00:44:05.760
but rather do creative,
interesting things,

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:08.000
you know, to try, just to make
it clear that they're real,

00:44:08.000 --> 00:44:10.071
and they have a character.

00:44:10.071 --> 00:44:12.411
Even their propaganda
directed against the Russians

00:44:12.411 --> 00:44:13.653
is often very interesting

00:44:13.653 --> 00:44:18.233
or even has a kind of almost
humanitarian tone to it.

00:44:18.233 --> 00:44:20.369
So the Ukrainians have done a better job,

00:44:20.369 --> 00:44:23.970
but the larger, but the
larger issue here is reality.

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:26.420
You know, can we talk to Russians

00:44:26.420 --> 00:44:28.930
and just help them
understand what's going on

00:44:28.930 --> 00:44:30.965
without brow beating them?

00:44:30.965 --> 00:44:34.069
You know, can we get to our
Russian friends and colleagues

00:44:34.069 --> 00:44:34.950
or their colleagues or our Russian cousins

00:44:34.950 --> 00:44:38.430
and their cousins, and just
communicate a little bit?

00:44:38.430 --> 00:44:40.520
Is the west going to do a better job

00:44:40.520 --> 00:44:42.680
at talking about what's actually going on?

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:44.510
Which doesn't mean
making better propaganda?

00:44:44.510 --> 00:44:46.950
I don't think we win
with better propaganda.

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:48.370
I don't think, you know,

00:44:48.370 --> 00:44:49.847
I don't think that's really our job,

00:44:49.847 --> 00:44:52.030
but can reality as such
hold back unreality.

00:44:52.030 --> 00:44:55.690
That's a big, so if this
thing only comes to a close,

00:44:55.690 --> 00:45:00.230
if we continue to keep a grip
on what's actually happening

00:45:00.230 --> 00:45:01.063
on the battlefield.

00:45:01.063 --> 00:45:02.962
That's a big part of it.

00:45:02.962 --> 00:45:05.510
If we drift off into
propaganda stereotypes

00:45:05.510 --> 00:45:06.840
or wishful thinking, you know,

00:45:06.840 --> 00:45:09.410
or we spend our time
talking about the concepts

00:45:09.410 --> 00:45:11.952
that Mr. Putin wants us to talk about.

00:45:11.952 --> 00:45:14.211
You know, then this goes on, right?

00:45:14.211 --> 00:45:16.370
So I just, I think it can, I mean,

00:45:16.370 --> 00:45:17.648
I don't wanna dodge the question, Jim.

00:45:17.648 --> 00:45:18.849
I do think this can end.

00:45:18.849 --> 00:45:21.660
I mean, I can imagine an
arrangement whereby this would end,

00:45:21.660 --> 00:45:24.729
but like everything else,
it depends like, you know,

00:45:24.729 --> 00:45:26.510
like everything else, like
this conversation itself,

00:45:26.510 --> 00:45:29.790
it depends upon the Ukrainians
continuing to fight, right?

00:45:29.790 --> 00:45:31.300
I mean, imagine a world

00:45:31.300 --> 00:45:33.628
in which the Ukrainians hadn't fought.

00:45:33.628 --> 00:45:34.911
It's a very different world, right?

00:45:34.911 --> 00:45:36.620
But if the Ukrainians hadn't fought,

00:45:36.620 --> 00:45:38.130
we would not be thinking about

00:45:39.010 --> 00:45:40.728
all the things we're
thinking right now, right?

00:45:40.728 --> 00:45:41.700
If the Ukrainians hadn't fought

00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:44.270
that wouldn't make Putin a better dictator

00:45:44.270 --> 00:45:46.140
or the Russian system, a better system.

00:45:46.140 --> 00:45:48.840
We just wouldn't be thinking
about it in the same way.

00:45:49.691 --> 00:45:51.445
If the Ukrainians hadn't fought, you know,

00:45:51.445 --> 00:45:52.410
people around the world
wouldn't have chance

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:54.922
to think about what they, you know,

00:45:54.922 --> 00:45:56.800
to think about what colonization
and decolonization mean,

00:45:56.800 --> 00:45:59.360
or to think about what democracy
and oligarchy mean, right?

00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:01.771
They've given us this chance,

00:46:01.771 --> 00:46:03.220
including the chance to
have the conversation

00:46:03.220 --> 00:46:04.610
that we're having right now,

00:46:04.610 --> 00:46:07.029
but for there to be some
kind of a settlement,

00:46:07.029 --> 00:46:10.351
I mean, there's, Arne's already
mentioned all the parts.

00:46:10.351 --> 00:46:11.420
The Ukrainians have to keep fighting.

00:46:11.420 --> 00:46:13.080
People have to keep helping them.

00:46:13.080 --> 00:46:15.810
There has to be a conversation,
if that's the word,

00:46:15.810 --> 00:46:17.500
among Russian elites.

00:46:17.500 --> 00:46:18.750
There has to be resistance

00:46:18.750 --> 00:46:21.229
on the part of the Russian population,

00:46:21.229 --> 00:46:23.870
and big powers like China and
America have to keep nudging

00:46:23.870 --> 00:46:25.547
and suggesting possible ways out.

00:46:27.290 --> 00:46:28.410
- Can I add something on that?

00:46:28.410 --> 00:46:29.560
- [Jim] Please, please.

00:46:30.630 --> 00:46:32.780
- So I just wanted to add that even after,

00:46:32.780 --> 00:46:36.090
so I've been in the prediction
business for a couple years,

00:46:36.090 --> 00:46:37.948
so I won't speak to speak to that part,

00:46:37.948 --> 00:46:42.746
but even after the Russian
troops withdraw from Ukraine,

00:46:42.746 --> 00:46:44.790
this will not be over for Ukrainians

00:46:44.790 --> 00:46:47.140
for a very, very long time.

00:46:47.140 --> 00:46:50.430
So I'm so glad to see so
many people here today.

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:52.110
We maxed out the attendance.

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:56.690
And so I want to ask you to
resist the urge to disengage

00:46:56.690 --> 00:46:58.290
because even in a week,

00:46:58.290 --> 00:47:00.040
I've seen a big difference in the way,

00:47:00.040 --> 00:47:03.140
I have the "New York Times"
sort of live stream updates

00:47:03.140 --> 00:47:05.520
on my computer, and I've
seen a big difference

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:07.450
in the way that they're covering things,

00:47:07.450 --> 00:47:09.410
even within a week.

00:47:09.410 --> 00:47:11.250
And I also forgot to mention,
as part of my background,

00:47:11.250 --> 00:47:13.352
I've been working with
the state department

00:47:13.352 --> 00:47:14.551
for about nine months now,

00:47:14.551 --> 00:47:16.632
tracking Russian disinformation efforts

00:47:16.632 --> 00:47:19.280
and synthesizing those
for government partners.

00:47:19.280 --> 00:47:22.849
So I have some background
there and can speak to that

00:47:22.849 --> 00:47:25.210
and can say that they're
actively right now

00:47:25.210 --> 00:47:27.250
pushing narratives and pushing efforts

00:47:27.250 --> 00:47:30.860
to try to get Western allies
to disengage from this issue.

00:47:30.860 --> 00:47:34.326
So just something to keep
in mind as this goes on,

00:47:34.326 --> 00:47:37.980
that's true for so many
tragedies and conflicts

00:47:37.980 --> 00:47:40.150
that happen around the world.

00:47:40.150 --> 00:47:43.006
It inevitably happens, but you know,

00:47:43.006 --> 00:47:45.272
there's been a hot war
raging in Eastern Ukraine

00:47:45.272 --> 00:47:49.160
for eight years, and the
west disengaged from it,

00:47:49.160 --> 00:47:50.510
really stopped covering it.

00:47:51.526 --> 00:47:53.093
And it's part of the reason
that we're here today.

00:47:53.093 --> 00:47:54.063
So just something to keep in mind.

00:47:54.948 --> 00:47:56.187
- Nellie, thanks.

00:47:56.187 --> 00:47:58.672
I'd like to open it up
with the remaining time

00:47:58.672 --> 00:48:00.339
for a few questions.

00:48:03.991 --> 00:48:04.824
Jimmy Hatch.

00:48:04.824 --> 00:48:07.158
- Hey, I'm a veteran and I served

00:48:07.158 --> 00:48:08.710
in a bunch of different
conflicts in the past

00:48:08.710 --> 00:48:09.563
in our history,

00:48:10.641 --> 00:48:12.930
and this reminds me particularly
of the former Yugoslavia.

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:15.883
And while I'm not culturally
aware of the Ukrainians,

00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:18.390
their history,

00:48:18.390 --> 00:48:20.280
I do think that there's the opportunity

00:48:20.280 --> 00:48:22.640
for what happened in the former Yugoslavia

00:48:22.640 --> 00:48:26.400
when the people who did
the bad things there,

00:48:26.400 --> 00:48:27.840
who created the mass graves,

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:30.630
they've concentrated everybody's
attention on Sarajevo.

00:48:30.630 --> 00:48:33.335
And during that time
they were doing things

00:48:33.335 --> 00:48:34.459
in the other part of the country.

00:48:34.459 --> 00:48:35.380
And I'm worried that that,

00:48:35.380 --> 00:48:37.335
for example, this morning,

00:48:37.335 --> 00:48:39.640
when I saw the news about the
nuclear planet being shelled,

00:48:39.640 --> 00:48:42.336
what do they not want us to see?

00:48:42.336 --> 00:48:44.280
And what do you think, how do you think

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:48.310
that they'll keep our attention focused

00:48:48.310 --> 00:48:51.642
and do something in a
different part of the country?

00:48:51.642 --> 00:48:53.003
What do we need to be looking for there?

00:48:57.220 --> 00:48:58.416
- Thank you.

00:48:58.416 --> 00:48:59.293
Are we doing this one by one or?

00:49:01.032 --> 00:49:01.865
- Yeah.

00:49:01.865 --> 00:49:02.880
- No, I think that's a
very apt observation.

00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:05.690
I mean, the Russians are
not on their best game

00:49:05.690 --> 00:49:07.860
with the propaganda
and the provocation yet

00:49:09.054 --> 00:49:11.550
because I believe their understanding,

00:49:11.550 --> 00:49:14.601
their expectation was that
Ukraine was gonna fold

00:49:14.601 --> 00:49:15.510
within 24 hours.

00:49:15.510 --> 00:49:17.910
And that, we think that
not just because of how,

00:49:18.764 --> 00:49:20.560
of how they set up, quote unquote,

00:49:20.560 --> 00:49:22.440
the first stage of the operation,

00:49:22.440 --> 00:49:24.598
but also because of some things

00:49:24.598 --> 00:49:26.981
that were accidentally
published in the Russian press,

00:49:26.981 --> 00:49:28.280
which basically, they
accidentally published

00:49:28.280 --> 00:49:30.841
some of their victory declarations,

00:49:30.841 --> 00:49:34.050
which if you haven't seen
them are absolutely chilling

00:49:34.050 --> 00:49:36.120
because they basically speak of a,

00:49:36.120 --> 00:49:39.710
of what, you know, what Raphael
Lemkin called the genocide

00:49:40.721 --> 00:49:42.620
in which the nation has been decapitated,

00:49:42.620 --> 00:49:45.420
and everyone else just has
no choice but to go along,

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:48.203
and Ukraine has essentially
ceased to exist.

00:49:49.530 --> 00:49:52.590
So they're not on, they're not,

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:55.750
in terms of provocation and
ideology and disinformation,

00:49:55.750 --> 00:49:58.626
I think it's only going to get stronger

00:49:58.626 --> 00:49:59.950
as I think maybe Nellie was suggesting

00:49:59.950 --> 00:50:02.460
as they try to, 'cause they
went in doing one thing.

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:04.070
That didn't work, and now
they're still, I think,

00:50:04.070 --> 00:50:05.760
trying to figure out how
they're gonna talk about

00:50:05.760 --> 00:50:06.593
what they're doing,

00:50:06.593 --> 00:50:08.230
and whether it's hard
for them to talk about it

00:50:08.230 --> 00:50:10.250
'cause they're not really sure themselves.

00:50:10.250 --> 00:50:12.680
It's Putin doubling down,
and they have to win,

00:50:12.680 --> 00:50:16.100
but what winning means, they don't know.

00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:18.010
And of course, I agree with
Arne, like it's gonna be,

00:50:18.010 --> 00:50:19.650
I don't think they're gonna win ever.

00:50:19.650 --> 00:50:21.410
But you know, the question is whether,

00:50:21.410 --> 00:50:23.897
the question is whether
there's some kind of

00:50:23.897 --> 00:50:26.230
acceptable solution in
months, you know, or whether,

00:50:26.230 --> 00:50:30.238
or whether it's like
Afghanistan plus for them

00:50:30.238 --> 00:50:32.963
in years and horror for the Ukrainians.

00:50:33.920 --> 00:50:37.240
But I mean, what I think is real

00:50:37.240 --> 00:50:40.580
is the attempt to assassinate
the Ukrainian leadership,

00:50:40.580 --> 00:50:43.950
and that's something which is
difficult to keep your eye on.

00:50:43.950 --> 00:50:46.030
But I think important not to forget about.

00:50:46.030 --> 00:50:49.600
And I don't think the Russians
have forgotten about it.

00:50:49.600 --> 00:50:53.650
That this war was about
decapitation, announced as such

00:50:54.922 --> 00:50:56.398
pretty much, pretty explicitly.

00:50:56.398 --> 00:50:57.890
We should probably, you
know, probably remember that.

00:50:57.890 --> 00:51:00.670
And if Ukrainian leaders,
you know, disappear

00:51:00.670 --> 00:51:02.755
or whatever it might be,

00:51:02.755 --> 00:51:05.597
there's a pretty strong
presumption about what might,

00:51:05.597 --> 00:51:06.897
what is in fact happening.

00:51:07.741 --> 00:51:09.830
You know, and that, I
mean, that Putin's concept

00:51:09.830 --> 00:51:11.940
is that Ukraine doesn't exist.

00:51:11.940 --> 00:51:15.250
There are just a few thousand,
you know, artificial,

00:51:15.250 --> 00:51:17.660
like when those people start
disappearing, you know,

00:51:17.660 --> 00:51:20.641
we can, I mean, that's just
a horrible thing to say,

00:51:20.641 --> 00:51:23.541
but that plan has already, has
essentially been announced.

00:51:27.820 --> 00:51:32.157
- [Jim] Please, and do
you need the microphone?

00:51:32.157 --> 00:51:32.990
- [Woman] Yes.

00:51:32.990 --> 00:51:36.233
- [Jim] So that the live,
the streaming can catch it.

00:51:38.310 --> 00:51:41.470
- First, I want say thank you
to the panelists and everyone

00:51:41.470 --> 00:51:42.303
for coming out.

00:51:43.192 --> 00:51:45.710
It's been really
instructive and eye opening

00:51:45.710 --> 00:51:48.920
to kind of hear your perspectives,
and I'm very appreciate

00:51:48.920 --> 00:51:52.603
as just like a passive American
observer of what's going on.

00:51:54.040 --> 00:51:57.970
I have seen sort of several
narratives put forth

00:51:57.970 --> 00:51:59.493
in the last week

00:51:59.493 --> 00:52:03.120
that perhaps some of what is
motivating Russia right now

00:52:03.120 --> 00:52:07.570
to kind of get themselves into this mess

00:52:07.570 --> 00:52:11.520
beyond just sort of the
ideological state defining stuff

00:52:13.810 --> 00:52:18.810
is actually like sort of maybe
also a battle over resources,

00:52:18.992 --> 00:52:21.020
specifically oil and natural gas,

00:52:21.020 --> 00:52:24.112
with the natural gas
found in the Black Sea.

00:52:24.112 --> 00:52:28.310
But just prior to the 2014 invasion,

00:52:28.310 --> 00:52:31.329
which Russia control Crimea now,

00:52:31.329 --> 00:52:33.383
that that would be the main way

00:52:33.383 --> 00:52:34.480
that Ukraine could have exploited

00:52:34.480 --> 00:52:36.880
those natural gas resources,

00:52:36.880 --> 00:52:39.946
but then there's still
some that are available

00:52:39.946 --> 00:52:41.296
from Odessa and other ports

00:52:42.130 --> 00:52:43.900
that Ukraine still
controls in the Black Sea.

00:52:43.900 --> 00:52:48.168
And I was, and I guess, so
if Ukraine were to exploit

00:52:48.168 --> 00:52:49.748
these natural gas resources

00:52:49.748 --> 00:52:52.370
and become the new Eastern
European petrol state,

00:52:52.370 --> 00:52:55.720
which theoretically could
threaten the Russian economy,

00:52:55.720 --> 00:53:00.313
which is primarily based on the
exploitation of fossil fuels

00:53:01.250 --> 00:53:04.909
and the sale of them to
the European continent.

00:53:04.909 --> 00:53:08.170
I was just wondering if you experts

00:53:08.170 --> 00:53:10.140
think there's any validity to that theory

00:53:10.140 --> 00:53:14.140
or if it's sort of maybe
sideshow distraction or yeah.

00:53:15.280 --> 00:53:17.670
Just anything you might speak
about that sort of energy

00:53:17.670 --> 00:53:19.782
and resource side of things.

00:53:19.782 --> 00:53:20.615
Thank you.

00:53:22.359 --> 00:53:26.460
- So I don't think that's
one of the key reasons

00:53:26.460 --> 00:53:29.133
why Putin has acted the way he has.

00:53:30.230 --> 00:53:33.080
We know again from some
of these leaked documents

00:53:33.080 --> 00:53:36.060
that Tim referred to that

00:53:36.060 --> 00:53:39.420
in part of how this is being presented,

00:53:39.420 --> 00:53:42.140
was supposed to be presented after victory

00:53:42.140 --> 00:53:45.050
from the Russian side, then the idea was

00:53:45.050 --> 00:53:48.737
that this would strengthen
this new cohesion

00:53:48.737 --> 00:53:51.860
that had been created
around Russia economically

00:53:51.860 --> 00:53:53.710
also with regard to access to resources,

00:53:53.710 --> 00:53:55.650
which then in Putin's telling,

00:53:55.650 --> 00:53:58.238
the west was trying to cut off

00:53:58.238 --> 00:53:59.662
in order to strangle Russia.

00:53:59.662 --> 00:54:02.559
I mean entire nonsense, of
course, with regard to it.

00:54:02.559 --> 00:54:05.676
But so I don't think that's
what we should be looking at

00:54:05.676 --> 00:54:06.681
most closely.

00:54:06.681 --> 00:54:08.220
I think the, as Tim has been saying,

00:54:08.220 --> 00:54:13.220
the central idea here is about
the attempted destruction

00:54:13.530 --> 00:54:15.970
of Ukrainian nationhood.

00:54:15.970 --> 00:54:19.270
And that is a story that
is first and foremost

00:54:19.270 --> 00:54:24.270
Putin's story about Russia
and about the kind of Russia

00:54:24.316 --> 00:54:26.913
that he wants to see
projected into the future.

00:54:28.097 --> 00:54:31.300
And this is where some of the
dissonance comes up as well.

00:54:31.300 --> 00:54:34.093
I mean, I do think that is actually quite,

00:54:35.061 --> 00:54:37.112
quite important to note here.

00:54:37.112 --> 00:54:39.177
And I really appreciated what Tim said

00:54:39.177 --> 00:54:42.190
about this being a very
novel way of thinking about

00:54:42.190 --> 00:54:45.453
how to rule, how to rule Ukraine.

00:54:45.453 --> 00:54:46.979
I hadn't given enough thought to that.

00:54:46.979 --> 00:54:48.683
And I think that's a
really, really good point,

00:54:49.840 --> 00:54:52.700
but it also is a way of
thinking that it seems to me

00:54:53.701 --> 00:54:56.220
is outta whack with almost
everything that is happening

00:54:56.220 --> 00:54:58.400
elsewhere in the world.

00:54:58.400 --> 00:55:01.340
And getting back to the
Chinese and Asian side,

00:55:01.340 --> 00:55:05.480
this is the kind of thing that
I think is really difficult

00:55:05.480 --> 00:55:08.800
for their elites to
get their heads around.

00:55:08.800 --> 00:55:12.480
I mean, they don't like
us very much necessarily.

00:55:12.480 --> 00:55:15.197
They don't like the way the
United States is governed

00:55:15.197 --> 00:55:17.480
or the way Europe is governed

00:55:17.480 --> 00:55:18.800
or anything of that sort.

00:55:18.800 --> 00:55:22.160
But there is still a, in
terms of conversations

00:55:22.160 --> 00:55:23.773
and in terms of discussions,

00:55:25.270 --> 00:55:26.640
as some of my Chinese friends like to say,

00:55:26.640 --> 00:55:28.993
we do speak the same language, right?

00:55:30.197 --> 00:55:31.897
In terms of the things that matter

00:55:31.897 --> 00:55:33.210
and the things that matter less.

00:55:33.210 --> 00:55:35.799
In terms of Putin's Russian elite,

00:55:35.799 --> 00:55:36.902
that is not the case.

00:55:36.902 --> 00:55:39.500
I mean, there is a kind of discourse

00:55:39.500 --> 00:55:42.510
that seem to be entirely internal

00:55:43.798 --> 00:55:47.118
to that particular elite

00:55:47.118 --> 00:55:49.860
and on the issue of resources, you know,

00:55:49.860 --> 00:55:52.040
I think in most other settings

00:55:52.999 --> 00:55:56.581
with this kind of effort being put into

00:55:56.581 --> 00:55:57.640
taking over another country

00:55:58.537 --> 00:56:00.170
that would have ranked
fairly highly up, you know,

00:56:00.170 --> 00:56:01.640
on the agenda.

00:56:01.640 --> 00:56:03.503
It doesn't in the Russian case.

00:56:04.580 --> 00:56:05.921
And the reason for that

00:56:05.921 --> 00:56:08.597
is that the discourse is different, right,

00:56:08.597 --> 00:56:10.599
from of what you find
in many other settings.

00:56:10.599 --> 00:56:12.023
At least that would be my response to it.

00:56:17.699 --> 00:56:21.339
- Yeah, I mean, American and
Russia are very different.

00:56:21.339 --> 00:56:24.915
But if you think back to the
US invasion of Iraq in 2003,

00:56:24.915 --> 00:56:26.620
anybody looking out of the
distance would've said,

00:56:26.620 --> 00:56:28.273
oh yeah, isn't that about the oil fields,

00:56:29.510 --> 00:56:33.320
but somehow it wasn't even
about the oil fields, right?

00:56:33.320 --> 00:56:34.153
It was about views about the world

00:56:34.153 --> 00:56:37.384
and how the world was gonna
change after a war and so on,

00:56:37.384 --> 00:56:40.420
which were entirely wrong, you know,

00:56:40.420 --> 00:56:41.983
just as these ideas are entirely wrong.

00:56:42.820 --> 00:56:45.053
I just wanted to, I wanted to, I agree,

00:56:46.133 --> 00:56:47.421
complete with what Arne said.

00:56:47.421 --> 00:56:48.254
I wanted though to tack onto your question

00:56:48.254 --> 00:56:50.840
a kind of larger
observation, which is that

00:56:52.500 --> 00:56:57.500
the 21st century is a century
of hydrocarbon oligarchy

00:56:59.218 --> 00:57:01.900
and getting to the 22nd century

00:57:01.900 --> 00:57:06.060
is gonna be a matter of breaking
up hydrocarbon oligarchy

00:57:06.060 --> 00:57:08.400
foreign and domestic,

00:57:08.400 --> 00:57:09.680
and this entire war wouldn't be possible

00:57:09.680 --> 00:57:12.540
without the way that the
Russian state is constructed

00:57:12.540 --> 00:57:15.598
as a hydrocarbon oligarchy.

00:57:15.598 --> 00:57:17.596
And, you know, I mean, of course,

00:57:17.596 --> 00:57:19.335
day to day, month to month,

00:57:19.335 --> 00:57:20.799
what we need to be thinking
about are the Ukrainians

00:57:20.799 --> 00:57:22.355
and supplying the Ukrainians.

00:57:22.355 --> 00:57:24.056
But this is, this whole war

00:57:24.056 --> 00:57:25.400
is actually actually one
more piece of evidence

00:57:25.400 --> 00:57:30.400
that we need to shift to clean
and safe sources of energy.

00:57:30.860 --> 00:57:33.850
That's so this isn't a distraction.

00:57:33.850 --> 00:57:34.730
I mean, we shouldn't be thinking of this

00:57:34.730 --> 00:57:37.537
as a distraction from climate change.

00:57:37.537 --> 00:57:39.280
This would be like one more reason why

00:57:39.280 --> 00:57:42.230
we need to be shifting over to
different sources of energy.

00:57:46.738 --> 00:57:49.197
- So I'm mindful of the time,

00:57:49.197 --> 00:57:51.260
and I'm mindful of the fact
that we're holding this

00:57:51.260 --> 00:57:56.210
at a day and time where classes
actually are in session.

00:57:56.210 --> 00:58:00.970
So with some regret, I'm
gonna call it to a close.

00:58:00.970 --> 00:58:03.438
We could talk for a long, long time.

00:58:03.438 --> 00:58:06.480
And as Nellie said, I hope we will be.

00:58:06.480 --> 00:58:10.410
I'd like to thank the panelists
for their time this morning.

00:58:10.410 --> 00:58:12.870
And most of all, I'd
like to thank all of you

00:58:12.870 --> 00:58:15.070
for taking time out of your day,

00:58:15.070 --> 00:58:19.330
showing the concern that's
evidenced by your being here

00:58:19.330 --> 00:58:20.533
on a Friday morning.

00:58:21.624 --> 00:58:22.982
Thank you so much.

00:58:22.982 --> 00:58:26.149
(audience applauding)

00:58:31.140 --> 00:58:33.723
(gentle music)

